n we were writing it  so  um that became the idea of someone  please save the city became part of that  that  crime yeah it was it was that time when  you know new yorkers were opening their  windows every night at 7 00 p.m and  banging pots and pans you know to say  thank you to you know all the frontline  workers and uh  i was gonna say i was curious too if if  there had ever been a conversation about  potentially sitting the song somewhere  else in the mcu but you would you mark  answered my question perfectly um you  know i  i'm sure fans would have been curious if  it had been more than one song like  seeing a potential you know first  avengers you know set tune or something  along those lines  right yeah yeah well yeah also but  captain america is a very he's he's got  such a beautiful story because it goes  back to the 40s and things like that so  yeah it could have been a hole  um  what was the process behind getting some  big broadway broadway folks involved  because you know adam's obviously  singing pretty heavily on the track so  was that kind of  was that a little bit more of like a  we're going to sneakily bring people in  you know keep everything under wraps it  was it was really just um  who can sing it yeah i can't sing i was  i found myself writing music that i  can't sing  we wrote  and then one before we continued with  the rest of the song we wanted to make  sure it was the right direction  so  the only person who could do vocal  vocally uh adapt to it was was uh with  adam i mean he came to mind adam  who i didn't i mean i had met him i  think once  really in passing and but like i i  texted our mutual friend seth rudetsky  who had done a whole broadway show with  him i said can you give me adam pascal's  phone number i'm going to text him to  see if and can he  belt a b-flat or a b and hang onto it  and seth was like are you crazy he can  literally hit any note and hang on to it  with or without vibrato whichever you  want so  i texted adam pascal out of the blue and  and said you know it's mark and scott  and they've asked us to write a song  that's basically like  the avengers musical and would you and  before i could even finish the question  he just texted yes  and you know he was at home as everyone  was and you know a lot of singers or  performers at that point had all gotten  their own microphones at home to try to  continue to work as much  as little or as much as was possible at  the time  so adam and then another friend named ty  taylor who i had only just sort of met  those are the first two guys i texted  who the only guys i knew who could  possibly hit all these notes and sing  with that kind of theatrical rock and  roll style  and they sang the whole song and demoed  it and those vocals that they recorded  in their home are are the vocals that  are on the recording even they're even a  little distorted here and there but we  didn't want to kind of like try to  capture  lightning in a bottle twice we so loved  what they had sung  and luckily they got to be in the actual  number and the filming  and there are a lot of other broadway  names who are only the voices of the  avengers because of kovid  uh they couldn't bring a whole bunch of  people you know flying people in was  very you know  they just couldn't do it with everyone  so it's very old-school movie musicals  where the people on screen singing  that's not really their voices i i don't  want to crush anyone's bubble  first anyone's mobile but  it's phenomenal singers like rory  donovan christopher sieber uh derek  klena  yeah shayna steele and bonnie milligan i  mean phenomenal singers every single one  of them  who and we were all so thrilled to be  doing it but we couldn't tell anybody  you know half a year of hanging on to  this so it was so thrilling when they  finally put the song that little bit in  that first trailer and then to see the  reaction of marvel fans  to it was just so  uh  thrilling it was thrilling yeah i got my  favorite compliment i've ever gotten was  somebody wrote it's slaps so i thought  that's better than a raven in new york  times isn't it that's that's the modern  day equivalent i think um  i would love to know the the  uh  thought process behind uh adding ant-man  to the song for the musical because it  was it just purely for the joke of you  know he wasn't even there but we're  going to stick him in anyway kind of  thing  makes clint go wait a second they didn't  get it right so but that was a marvel  request so you know that it's a perfect  example of the sense of humor they have  within the movies  and we also you know scott and i both  knew that i mean that was the main point  in this in the script  was that clint is dragged to the musical  avengers musical and that he is just  rolling his eyes at it going why am i  here and what is this what  this really happened to me and now it's  their singing and dancing and what's  ant-man doing in this you know so that  was all part of the script and we were  more than happy to oblige  uh it's hysterical that guy dancing as a  hand man  i think i have time for one more  question which is are you both prepared  for the possibility uh that this could  become a full musical in real life uh  and if so are you are you ready to come  back and and do the whole thing  yes we are equal to the challenge yes  if only kevin feige was on social media  i'd say  inundate him with requests i mean but a  lot of people are saying it so you know  marvel works in mysterious ways we'll  see what  um those are actually all the questions  i have but thank you so much for taking  the time to sit and chat it was it was a  blast and and the song's great and  honestly i hope i hope we'll get to see  a little more rogers the musical at some  point because it was a lot of fun  thank you thank you

